WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.657 --> 00:00:07.740
SOPHIA YANG: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.480
I'm Sophia, product
manager from Google Cloud.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.450
I'm here today to tell you
about Cloud Asset Inventory.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.850
We all know by bringing
workload to Cloud,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.060
you're benefiting from its
scalability, high performance,

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.670
secureness, and cost efficiency.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.750
Yet at the same time, admins,
especially platform admins,

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.990
security admins, are facing
never-before-seen challenges

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:32.450
that were brought in by the
large quantity of resources

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:36.060
and data that can be easily
created by developers.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.660
In fact, enterprises have
millions of resources

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.768
running on Google Cloud today.

00:00:40.768 --> 00:00:42.310
So how can you see
all the things you

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.440
have on Cloud at the same time?

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.510
How can you keep an
eye on all the changes

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.180
as they happen so you
don't miss a threat?

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.000
How do you answer questions that
seem simple but are actually

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.380
challenging, like how many
VMs do I have in my org?

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.630
Who can change my
billing accounts?

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:02.540
Or what service account keys
that are older than 60 days?

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.075
I'm very excited
to introduce you

00:01:04.075 --> 00:01:06.710
to Cloud Asset Inventory,
that aim to solve all

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.380
of these challenges for you.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.780
As you can see here,
Cloud Asset Inventory

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.510
is a fully managed
metadata service.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.980
So let's first address
the elephant in the room--

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.570
what is an asset?

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.830
An asset can be a resource,
like a VM instance, a BigQuery

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.280
table.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.680
It can also be a policy,
like IAM policy, org policy.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.080
It can also be VM
runtime information,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.620
like system updates.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.050
So now let's talk
about metadata.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.830
Metadata here means the
configuration of an asset, not

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.810
the actual [INAUDIBLE] content.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.750
For example, it is about
the name, the size,

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.990
creation time for the disk,
but not the files that

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.160
are written on the disks.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.130
It offers many
inventory services

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.140
on your entire GCP and
Anthos environment, which

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.540
makes it truly a one-stop shop.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.570
So you don't need to
go to Compute Engine

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.390
to find all your VMs and
then go to Cloud Storage

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.400
to see all your
buckets, for example.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.270
And what is more
convenient than that,

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.500
it works at the organization,
folder, and the project level,

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.810
with fine-grained
IAM policy controls.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.980
So regardless of your
permission level,

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.580
you can always take
full advantage of it.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.640
Lastly, you can get up to 35
days of full change history

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.010
from Cloud Asset Inventory.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.510
Now let's dive deeper
into the features.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.750
First and foremost,
you can get a snapshot

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.430
of your entire inventory.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.430
You can use either the
Export or the List service.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.450
The difference is that Export is
a long-running operation, which

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.780
will export your data to
either a Cloud Storage

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.900
Bucket or a BigQuery table,
which makes it easier for you

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.260
to directly query and
analyze data later.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.080
List API works just
like any other List API,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.040
except it supports a timestamp,
where all the data will

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.130
be included in the standard
HTTP response in case

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.420
you don't want to deal
with a storage location.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.030
You can also get a
full change history

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:02.260
by either using the UI or
API, which I'll demo later.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.030
Now, after getting
full visibility

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.520
for your entire
inventory plus history,

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.890
we also offer a real-time
notification feature for you

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.860
to easily achieve
continuous monitoring.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:15.490
It allows you to monitor assets,
and will send a Pub/Sub message

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.230
when change occurs.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.040
You can define your criteria
as a name, as a type,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.470
or by org, folder, and project.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:25.820
You can even use CEL condition.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.120
By the way, CEL is the
expression language

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.870
created by Google.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.200
For example, you can say, notify
me as soon as any of our rules

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.860
got changed in my
org, or notify me

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.760
only if IAM policy is changed
to be open, or one of the VMs

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.430
deleted.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.060
Here's an example scenario.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.540
An admin wants to
monitor and get

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.500
notified when a Gmail account
is added to an IAM policy.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.050
You first define your
condition in a real-time feed

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.190
using the code shown below.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.090
Now if a malicious user
adds his own Gmail account

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.370
into the policy,
Cloud Asset Inventory

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.680
will trigger a Cloud
Pub/Sub message right to you

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:01.940
in real-time.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.820
From there, you can define
all remediation actions.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.010
For example, you can
write a Cloud Function

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.730
to roll back the change or send
an email to project owners.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.600
The real-time
monitoring capability,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.230
especially the conditions
supported through CEL,

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.990
ensures that you won't
miss any important changes,

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.420
and also filters out
the noises so you

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.300
can stay focused and
keep your costs low.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:23.960
OK, now.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.300
If you're one of the
policy admins or privacy

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.150
admins who have to be
on top of access grants,

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.000
take care of access
certificate, and keep track

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.430
of all the changes
across a whole org,

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.330
you will be really excited to
learn a powerful tool offered

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.990
by Cloud Asset Inventory--

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.210
IAM Policy Analyzer.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.020
In a nutshell, it does deep and
thorough analysis on policies

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.900
and related resources to help
you answer the question, who

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.240
can access what and why?

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.460
For example, what
service accounts

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.280
can access my Buckets that
contain sensitive data,

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.940
or what access does this
terminated employee still have?

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.840
So what makes the tool powerful?

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.360
Let me show you with
an actual example.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.690
Who can change my
firewall rules?

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.715
This is one of the top
questions admin need

00:05:07.715 --> 00:05:09.740
to answer and keep track to.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.320
Well, it turns out you have
to peel through many layers

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.840
to actually get to
the full picture,

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.080
starting from the "who" part.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.130
"Who" can be a user, a group,
or even a service account.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:23.840
In the case of group, there can
be multiple layers of groups,

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.690
which we call nested groups.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:27.490
In case of a service
account, there

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.620
can also be multiple
layers of service accounts

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:31.990
that can impersonate
one another.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.960
Policy Analyzer can handle
all of the above to give you

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.270
a complete picture
on the "who" part.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:42.200
Now let's look at what's needed
for changing a firewall rule.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.980
Turns out three permissions--
create, update, or delete--

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.040
can all make changes in
firewalls in some ways.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.560
Policy Analyzer is capable
of mapping IAM rules

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.910
to permissions for both
curated roles and custom roles.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.840
At last, let's look
at the last part

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.360
of the question,
project A. We all

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.160
know IAM policies
can be inherited,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.400
which requires you to not
only look at IAM policy

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.020
through our client
project A here,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.290
but also trace up along the
hierarchy to the folders

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:12.380
and to the orgs above to
make sure all of the policies

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:13.550
are analyzed.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.800
Luckily, Policy
Analyzer analyzes

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:19.350
resource and the policy
hierarchy as well.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.610
It also analyzes IAM
conditions and exports results

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.860
to Cloud Storage Buckets
or BigQuery table.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:26.760
So here you go.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.750
You can see how much work
the Policy Analyzer does

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:31.410
for you behind
the scene in order

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.700
to provide a truly complete
and comprehensive answer on who

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.190
can access what and why.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.502
Now it's the demo time.

00:06:38.502 --> 00:06:40.460
As I mentioned earlier,
let's take a quick look

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.310
at the Cloud Asset
Inventory Developer Console.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:45.320
The Cloud Asset Inventory
Developer Console

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:48.140
is located under the
IAM and Admin tab.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.700
First you can use
a tag on the top

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.100
to switch between org, folder,
or project that you would

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.430
like to see your assets at.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.080
Once landed on the
landing page, you

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.060
will see an Overview
tab with automatically

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.130
pre-populated dashboards for
your deployment insights;

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:06.690
also resource and policy tabs
for details about assets.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.450
The first geograph shows you
how your zonal and regional

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.160
resources are located
across the globe.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.060
You can also get to
a table view of it.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.550
The next one shows how
your VM instance counts

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:19.162
have been changing over time.

00:07:19.162 --> 00:07:21.120
And the next view shows
you the resource counts

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.755
by services and asset types.

00:07:23.755 --> 00:07:25.380
You can also use the
predefined filters

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.150
on the left to
focus only on assets

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.450
that you are interested
in, like VM instances.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.430
As you can see, I
have 17, and they all

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.650
live in a US Central region.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.030
Click anywhere on
the dashboard--

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.430
it will take you to the
corresponding resource IAM

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:39.610
Policy tab.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:42.060
You can see here all the
filter options are carried over

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.940
from the graph setting
before, so let's uncheck them.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:48.930
Let's assume I want to search
for certain VM instances.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.240
So I can first check
on the asset type here.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.820
And then I will further
refine my search results

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.130
by typing the search
term into the search bar,

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.640
like name contains
the instance and label

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.370
key contains color group.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:03.900
And here are my results.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.710
I can also search by directly
typing in certain asset

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.640
attribute values, like a
[INAUDIBLE] IP, as that's

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.150
what I found on
the internet and I

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.750
want to locate which VM it is.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:16.890
And here you go.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:18.480
I found my VM.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:21.480
I can then click on the
instance to see the Detail page.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:23.910
You can see the
high-level summary first.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:27.070
Also, if Compute Engine
VM Manager is enabled,

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.250
you can see the runtime
information as well,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:33.890
like OS version, kernel,
running or stopped.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.419
You can go to the
Change History tab

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:39.200
to see the full change
history up to 35 days.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.809
You can see here all
the timestamp shows

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:46.010
ones that had a metadata change,
and I can choose two timestamps

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.720
to compare the differences.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.990
In this case, I can see that
a label, color group pink,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.720
was removed between the
two versions, as well

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.400
as some other change.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.530
Next, you can go to
the Full Metadata tab

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:00.770
for all the details,
like description, disk,

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.750
service account, et cetera.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.580
And lastly, you can
see the IAM policy

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:08.460
that's attached to the
resource in the IAM Policy tab.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.980
That is all for today, and
we encourage all of you

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.450
to try it out.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:15.150
Check out the documentation
product pages down below.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.765
And we're looking forward
to hearing from all of you.

00:09:17.765 --> 00:09:19.140
Thank you very
much for watching.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

